{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.SchurWeyl.SytContentTuple","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SytContentTuple","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells","lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.JucysMurphyOV","lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.SytMathlibBridge"],"declarations":2,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.syt_eq_of_cellContent_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.syt_eq_of_cellContent_eq","kind":"theorem","line":68,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SytContentTuple.lean#L68-L89","doc":"**Separation — the content tuple determines the tableau** (`cellOf` form).  Two standard\ntableaux of the same shape that assign the same content to every label `1 ≤ k ≤ |μ|` are equal.\n\nThis is `QOU.BraidKnot.JucysMurphy.syt_content_tuple_injective` transported across the bridge.\nThe mathematical content is entirely there: entries increase strictly along diagonals, so the\n*row* of the cell holding `k` is a function of the content tuple alone, and row + content gives\nthe column. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.cellOf_eq_tableauCellEquiv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.cellOf_eq_tableauCellEquiv","kind":"theorem","line":50,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SytContentTuple.lean#L50-L66","doc":"**The label correspondence across the SYT bridge.**  `tableauCellEquiv` finds the cell of\n`T.toBraidKnot` carrying label `s`; since `toBraidKnot` shifts every on-cell entry down by one,\nthat is the cell of `T` carrying `s + 1`, i.e. `cellOf T (s + 1)`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}